<spell-page "site-tabs usage" notebook>
	<header level="1">
		Site tabs
		<header>Site tabs show the page's family as a row of tabs:  the parent page first, the page shown selected, each a link.</>
	</>
	<section>
		<toolbar class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Hand it the same `pages` you hand `<ui-site-nav>`, and put it above the page's content.

			- `pages`: each page's `title` and `href`, its `pages` under it.
			  A parent page's `tab` is what its tab says, when not its `title`.
			- `current` says which page is shown, by its `href` or its `id`; without it, the page at the browser's address.
			- A parent with no `href` only holds pages, so it has no tab, and a page whose address has a parameter
			  (`/films/:film`) isn't one either. A family of fewer than two tabs draws nothing.
			- Picking a tab goes there. A site that shows its pages itself cancels `ui-navigating` and shows
			  `event.detail.page`. A middle click or Command-click opens a new tab, as any link does.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			document.querySelector("ui-site-tabs").addEventListener("ui-navigating", (event) => {
			  event.preventDefault()
			  showPage(event.detail.page)
			})
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			Each tab is a link: Tab moves between them, Enter goes there. In a row that scrolls, focusing a tab scrolls it into sight.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			The row is a `nav` landmark named `label`, else the family's parent page's title. The selected tab is
			`aria-current="page"`. Without a family nothing is drawn, so nothing is announced.
		</>
	</>
</spell-page>
